    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mohists taught impartial care: a person should care equally for all others, whatever their relationship, which set them against the Confucians, who held that love should be unconditional but not indiscriminate.</li><li>Mohists formed a disciplined organisation with units in all the major kingdoms and became the pre-eminent siege-defence engineers before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> unification, opposing offensive war but accepting force in legitimate defence.</li><li>Mohism almost disappeared as an independent school after 221 BCE; the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy suggests that its more appealing ideas were shared with rival schools by the middle of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the story of Gongshu in the Mohist canon, Mozi travelled ten days and nights to Chu, made a wall of his belt and sticks to stand for machines, and showed Gongshu Pan that he could defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> against any attack; he then announced that three hundred of his disciples were already on Song’s walls, and the king cancelled the invasion.</p></aside>
    </section>

