    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mohism was an ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-philosophy">Chinese philosophy</a> of ethics and logic, rational thought and scientific technology, developed by the scholars who studied under the philosopher Mozi, who lived from about 470 BCE to about 391 BCE. It is embodied in an eponymous book, the Mozi. It arose at about the same time as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legalism">Legalism</a>, and was one of the four main philosophic schools of the period from around 770 to 221 BCE, the Spring and Autumn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States periods</a>. In that time Mohism was seen as a major rival to Confucianism.</p><p>Mozi developed the concept of ai, usually rendered as love, in the 4th century BCE as a reaction to Confucianism’s benevolent love. He tried to replace what he considered a long-entrenched Chinese over-attachment to family and clan. Other features of his thought answered conditions of his age: war and famine were common, and it is suggested that Mozi spoke against lavish funerals and music in response to the Zhou king and the aristocrats, who spent great time on delicate music while ordinary peasants could hardly meet their subsistence needs.</p><p>The Mohists also formed a structured organisation, a network of local units in all the major kingdoms made up of both the scholarly and the working classes, each led by a juzi, literally “chisel”, who appointed his own successor.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mohism is best known for the concept popularly translated as “universal love” (兼愛, jiān ài). According to Edward Craig, “impartial care” is the more accurate translation, because Mozi was more concerned with ethics than with morality. A person should care equally for all other individuals, whatever their relationship to them, and the Mohists held that all people are equally deserving of material benefit and protection from harm. Morality was defined not by tradition and ritual but by a constant guide that parallels <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utilitarianism">utilitarianism</a>, one that promotes behaviour maximising the general utility of all the people in a society.</p><p>The basic goods in this consequentialist thinking, in the words quoted from a modern scholar, are order, material wealth and an increase in population. Mozi posited that conflict arises from the absence of moral uniformity, so leaders were to be chosen who would surround themselves with righteous followers and create a hierarchy that harmonises what is right and wrong, with conformity in speech and behaviour expected of all. This model leaves no freedom of speech, but subjects were required to report all things good or bad to their rulers. Mozi opposed nepotism and held that an incapable officer should be demoted, even a relative of the ruler. Mohism was opposed to any aggression, especially war between states, though a state might use force in legitimate defence.</p><p>Mohists believed in Heaven (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a>) as a divine force, in spirits that knew of the immoral acts of men and punished them, and in austerity in life and government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-needham"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-needham.jpg" alt="Joseph Needham" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-needham">Joseph Needham</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Needham_in_Cambridge_1965_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kognos, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mohism was popular in early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Mohists were often hired by the warring kingdoms as advisors to the state, and like the Confucians they hired out their services not only for gain but to realise their own ethical ideals. In this they resembled the other wandering philosophers and knights-errant of the period. They believed in aiding the defensive warfare of smaller Chinese states against the offensive warfare of larger domineering states.</p><p>Within each unit of the organisation a frugal and ascetic lifestyle was enforced. The Mohists developed the sciences of fortification and statecraft and wrote treatises on government, from efficient agricultural production to the laws of inheritance. Their understanding of mathematics and the physical sciences, combined with an anti-militarist philosophy and the skills of artisans, made them the pre-eminent siege-defence engineers before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> unification of China, and followers were employed for their ability as negotiators and as defence engineers.</p><p>One offshoot that has received some attention is the School of Names, which was interested in resolving logical puzzles. Little of its writing survives, since problems of logic were deemed trivial by most later Chinese philosophers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-needham">Joseph Needham</a> saw the group as developing a precursor philosophy of science that was never fully developed, while others believe that calling these Logicians proto-scientists reveals too much modern bias.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/the-art-of-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-art-of-war.jpg" alt="The Art of War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/the-art-of-war">The Art of War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamboo_book_-_unfolded_-_UCR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">vlasta2, bluefootedbooby on flickr.com, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mohism was not the doctrine of a state, and its record in practice is that of its organisation and its engineers. Mozi and his disciples worked concertedly and systematically to invent and synthesise measures of benefit to defence, including defensive arms and strategy, and their corresponding logistics and military mobilisation. Many were applied and remained an aspect of military affairs throughout history. The Mozi is highly respected by modern scholars and ranks as a classic on military matters on a par with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu">Sunzi</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-art-of-war">Art of War</a>, the former on defensive strategy, the latter on offensive strategy.</p><p>The story of Gongshu in the Mohist canon dramatises this work. When Mozi heard that Gongshu Pan had built machines for the king of Chu to use in an invasion of the smaller state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, he travelled ten days and nights to Chu, made a wall of his belt and sticks to stand for the machines, and showed that he could defend Song against any offensive strategy of Chu. He announced that three hundred of his disciples were already on the walls of Song, and the king cancelled the invasion.</p><p>The Mohist canon also described many fields of physical science and gave a small wealth of information on mathematics, with definitions of the point, parallels, circumference, diameter, radius and volume.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mohist impartiality was a target of attack by other Chinese philosophical schools, most notably the Confucians, who believed that while love should be unconditional it should not be indiscriminate: children, for example, should hold a greater love for their parents than for random strangers. The Mohists answered that people in principle should care for all people equally and that love should be offered without regard to reciprocation.</p><p>Modern readers have raised other doubts. The unity in belief and speech Mozi sought was meant to unite people and avoid sectarianism, but that in a situation of corruption and tyranny the teaching might be misused as a tool for oppression. The Mohist model also has no freedom of speech. The Mohists were wary of more atheistic thinkers of the time, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a>, and, given the vague nature of the records, the Mohist scribes themselves may not have been clear about spirits.</p><p>Mozi argued against fatalism, which he blamed for poverty and suffering, using three criteria: history, the experiences of common people and usefulness in law or politics. He also took whole chapters, titled “Against Music”, to argue that the fine arts had no utility in governing or for the common people. The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy calls the distinctively Mohist package of economic and cultural views, including its parsimony and its rejection of music and ritual, harsh and unappealing.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> after 221 BCE, China was no longer divided into states constantly fighting one another, and the Mohist skill in defending cities in siege wars was no longer needed. Mohism almost disappeared as an independent school of thought, transformed and integrated into sects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>. The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy gives a further reason: that as a social and philosophical movement Mohism gradually collapsed into irrelevance, since by the middle of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> its more appealing aspects were shared with rival schools. Its core ethical doctrines had largely been absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> in a modified and unsystematic form, and the difficult, dense texts of the later Mohist Canons quickly became corrupt.</p><p>Modern scholars have drawn lessons from its fate. Jin Guantao, Fan Hongye and Liu Qingfeng have argued that without the influence of the proto-scientific precepts of Mohism, Chinese science lacked a definitive structure. They hold that had Mohism, rich in scientific thought, grown and strengthened in the Eastern Han period, when Han studies of the Confucian classics were declining, conditions might have favoured a scientific structure, but that this did not happen.</p><p>Mohism has been set beside later ideas of ethics: its tenets of altruism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utilitarianism">utilitarianism</a> link it to the subjects of Consequentialism, Buddhist ethics and Effective altruism, and the book Mozi remains the text that embodies its doctrine.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mohism was an ancient Chinese school founded on the teaching of Mozi, built on impartial care for all people, merit in government and opposition to offensive war. Its organised followers became the foremost siege-defence engineers of their day, and the school almost disappeared after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> unification of 221 BCE.</em></p>

